\chapter{The math library}
The \api{math} package collects numeric operations outside the core operator
set. It exports \api{Abs}, \api{Floor}, \api{Ceil}, \api{Sqrt}, \api{Pow},
\api{Min}, \api{Max}, and the numeric constant \api{Pi}.

\begin{mullcode}
val M <- G:LoadLib("math") or G:DieWith("$E?");
G:Assert(M:Abs(-7) == 7);
G:Assert(M:Floor(-1.2) == -2);
G:Assert(M:Ceil(-1.2) == -1);
G:Assert(M:Sqrt(81) == 9);
G:Assert(M:Pow(2, 10) == 1024);
G:Assert(M:Min(3, 8) == 3);
G:Assert(M:Max(3, 8) == 8);
G:Assert(M:Pi > 3.14 and M:Pi < 3.15);
\end{mullcode}

The first four operations take one number. \api{Pow}, \api{Min}, and
\api{Max} take two. Every wrapper checks the argument count and numeric kind.
Results pass through MuLL's finite-number constructor, so an invalid square
root or an overflowing power raises an error instead of introducing NaN or
infinity into later calculations.

\section{Numerical expectations}
The implementation uses the host's binary64 math functions. Exact integer
examples are useful conformance tests; transcendental computations should
normally be compared with an appropriate tolerance. Equality itself remains
exact numeric equality because an implicit tolerance would make collection
equality and branching difficult to reason about.

For decimal money, arbitrary precision integers, or domain-specific units,
register a dedicated native library and define an explicit representation.
The core number kind is intentionally small and uniform across the two hosts.
